  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Uni-Stream: A Unified Streaming Speech Language Model for Speech Synthesis and Recognition",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32187",
      "description": "arXiv:2610.32187v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Unified streaming speech language models aim to support speech recognition and synthesis within a single model, enabling real-time human-computer interaction. However, jointly supporting these two directions of speech-text conversion while preserving task-specific performance remains challenging. We propose Uni-Stream, a unified streaming speech language model built on compact pretrained speech models. Uni-Stream processes interleaved text and speech in a streaming format and achieves performance comparable to task-specific models through two-stage training. We also develop a data annotation pipeline for streaming training and will publicly release the generated transcriptions and word-level timestamps. We further examine Uni-Stream's representations across tasks and modalities and find that speech and text residence in distinct latent feature space even when trained in a unified model architecture, while representations within each modality become more task-dependent in later layers.",
      "source_name": "arXiv AI",
      "published": "2026-10-26T04:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "X-Dubbing: Speech-to-Speech Translation under Temporal and Visual Constraints",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32042",
      "description": "arXiv:2610.32042v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Cross-lingual dubbing aims to convert source speech into target-language speech that remains synchronized with the original video. The key to this task is to produce a faithful translation that is naturally speakable within the source timing, which is non-trivial because languages differ in duration, rhythm, and articulation. In this paper, we propose X-Dubbing, which formulates the task as a joint problem of time-aware speech-to-text translation and video-aware text-to-speech. Specifically, our method consists of a Dubbing Translator that generates temporally structured scripts and a Voice Actor that renders them in synchrony with the source video, both grounded in a language-agnostic timing budget. We further refine the translator with dubbing-aware feedback from the synthesized speech, encouraging scripts that yield well-synchronized dubbing. This design requires no paired dubbing data, as the two components are trained on speech-text and monolingual audio-visual data respectively. Extensive experiments show that X-Dubbing outperforms recent baselines across diverse metrics, including isochrony and audio-visual correspondence.",
      "source_name": "arXiv Computer Vision",
      "published": "2026-10-26T04:00:00"
    }
  ]
